he domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D$.\n- Statement III is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186151932 :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-1,1] \\times [0,1]$, and\n$\\iint\\limits_D \\mid x-y \\mid \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A$,\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2616/Source/Images/8e46da282ec5e52c3797796b84fba298.png)\n\n1. ${\\begin{vmatrix}x - y &,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∖D; 0, \\mid (x, y) ∈ D∖R$\n2. ${0, \\mid (x, y) ∈ R∖D;\\begin{vmatrix}x - y &,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ D$\n3. ${\\begin{vmatrix}x - y &,\\end{vmatrix}(x, y) ∈ R∩D; 0, \\mid (x, y) ∉ D$"] [17592186151932 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2616/Source/Images/f75faf2d7defb332a3de4e495f71609f.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely.\n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\mid x-y \\mid$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mid x-y \\mid \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use function I. This functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,\\backslash D$. For example, $(x,y) = (1,0) \\in R\\,\\backslash\\, D$, but the function is nonzero at this point.\n- Functions II and III equal $\\mid x-y \\mid$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186151393 17592186151394 17592186151395 17592186151396 17592186151397 17592186151399 17592186151400 17592186151401 17592186151402 17592186151403 17592186151405 17592186151406 17592186151407 17592186151408 17592186151409] "immut